# PHASE 1 — CRITICAL ASSESSMENT REGRESSION INVESTIGATION

## Priority: P0

This is the most important part of the project.

The assessment submission system previously worked correctly.

Students could manually submit their assessments. When the timer reached zero, the assessment automatically expired, closed and submitted on behalf of the student.

The system also had fallback mechanisms and confirmation messages.

These behaviours must be restored.

Do not discard the existing implementation and create an unrelated replacement without first identifying the regression.

## 1.1 Investigate the JavaScript error

The supplied screenshot shows:

`MANUAL_DELIVERABLE_TYPES is not defined`

Investigate:

- Every reference to `MANUAL_DELIVERABLE_TYPES`.
- Where the constant or equivalent configuration is supposed to be declared.
- Whether it was renamed, deleted or moved.
- Whether its import or export is missing.
- Whether the script that defines it loads before the assessment script.
- Whether an older module is still referencing a removed variable.
- Whether a script error prevents other assessment event listeners from registering.
- Whether SPA navigation or dynamic script loading causes the declaration to become unavailable.

Fix the actual cause.

Do not merely suppress the error or create an arbitrary empty constant.

Preserve all existing manual-deliverable functionality and supported deliverable types.

## 1.2 Investigate manual submission

Trace the complete manual submission process:

1. Student opens an assessment.
2. Student answers questions.
3. Answers are saved.
4. Student selects Submit.
5. Confirmation is requested where appropriate.
6. Submission is processed.
7. Final answers are persisted.
8. Attempt status becomes submitted.
9. Submission timestamp is recorded.
10. Student receives the appropriate confirmation.
11. Grading and result-release processes continue.

Inspect the actual form submission handler, button event, asynchronous operations, validation logic and Firebase writes.

Check for:

- Incorrect button types.
- Missing event listeners.
- Broken form IDs.
- Event propagation issues.
- Incorrect disabled-button logic.
- Unhandled promises.
- Failed Firestore writes.
- Permission errors.
- Incorrect assessment status checks.
- Duplicate event listeners.
- Incorrect assessment identifiers.
- Race conditions.
- JavaScript errors occurring before the submission handler executes.

Restore the original functionality without introducing duplicate submission mechanisms.

## 1.3 Restore automatic submission at timeout

The previous automatic submission mechanism must continue working.

When the assessment timer reaches zero:

- The assessment must expire.
- The student must no longer be able to modify answers.
- The latest saved answers must be collected.
- The assessment must automatically submit on the student's behalf.
- The attempt must be marked as submitted or expired-submitted according to the existing data model.
- The submission time and reason must be recorded.
- The student must see the appropriate expiry and submission confirmation.
- Grading must proceed where applicable.

The assessment must not remain open indefinitely after the countdown reaches zero.

The timer reaching zero must trigger a real submission process, not merely change the displayed countdown.

Use the existing fallback mechanisms wherever possible.

If the student's internet connection is interrupted, implement safe recovery so that an attempt can be finalised without creating duplicate submissions or losing saved answers.

## 1.4 Make submission reliable

Use an authoritative server-side assessment deadline and a safe, idempotent submission operation.

The operation must:

- Prevent duplicate submissions.
- Handle simultaneous manual submission and timeout.
- Protect already-submitted attempts.
- Preserve the last confirmed answers.
- Record server timestamps.
- Handle network and database errors.
- Return a clear success or failure result.

The interface must not display a successful submission message before the submission has actually been persisted.

Saving progress and submitting an assessment must remain two different operations.

## 1.5 Preserve existing fallback messages

Find and preserve the messages already used by the application, including messages indicating:

- Assessment submitted successfully.
- Time expired.
- Assessment automatically submitted.
- Progress saved.
- Submission failed and can be retried, where recovery is possible.

Do not unnecessarily rewrite existing student-facing messages.

## 1.6 Important regression scenario

Reproduce the supplied screenshot scenario:

- Student has answered 100 out of 100 questions.
- Timer reaches 00:00.
- Progress is already saved.
- The student remains on the assessment screen.

The corrected system must automatically finalise that attempt, close the assessment and show a confirmation.

Also test when fewer than all questions have been answered.

