# PHASE 7 — ADMINISTRATOR HIERARCHY AND ROLE-BASED ACCESS CONTROL

## Priority: P0

The administrative system needs a proper hierarchy.

The Super Admin must have unrestricted access to all legitimate administrative functions.

Other administrators must only have access to the functions assigned to their respective roles.

## 7.1 Super Admin

The Super Admin must be able to:

- Access every administrative module.
- View all relevant records.
- Manage users.
- Manage administrators.
- Manage tutors.
- Manage schools.
- Manage students.
- Manage parents.
- Manage assessments.
- Manage attendance.
- Manage notifications.
- Access financial records.
- Manage system configuration.
- Manage role permissions.
- Access system-wide activity logs.

The Super Admin must not be restricted by ordinary sub-admin permissions.

## 7.2 Sub-admins

Sub-admins must receive specific permissions.

Examples of manageable permission categories:

- Content management.
- Academic administration.
- Student administration.
- School administration.
- Financial administration.
- Tutor administration.
- Notification management.
- Reporting.
- Other existing administrative responsibilities.

A sub-admin must only see the dashboard tabs relevant to their assigned responsibilities.

They must not see unrelated administrative tabs or have access to restricted operations.

The permission system should be configurable by the Super Admin.

## 7.3 Other roles

Preserve the existing tutor, school administrator, parent and student roles.

Their access should be limited by their responsibilities and relationships.

A tutor sees their assigned students, classes and schools.

A School Admin sees their own school's information.

A parent sees their enrolled children.

A student sees their own records.

## 7.4 Backend permission enforcement

This requirement is especially important.

Hiding a navigation tab does not provide security.

Every protected operation must be checked independently through appropriate backend logic and Firestore security rules.

Investigate and prevent:

- Privilege escalation.
- Users modifying their own roles.
- Unauthorised direct database reads.
- Unauthorised direct database writes.
- Cross-school data exposure.
- Access to restricted administrative routes.
- Unauthorised assessment or attendance modifications.

Use the existing authentication and role system as the starting point.

Do not create conflicting role definitions across different portal files.

